Bug: when there's only one attachment with the given name, `!has_multiple` short-circuits to `return Ok(first_match)` — completely ignoring a user-provided `--offset`. So `mcap get attachment demo.mcap --name a --offset 999` silently returns the attachment at whatever offset it actually lives at, with no error or warning.

If the user explicitly passes `--offset`, they either expect that exact offset or an error. Suggested fix:

```rust
if !has_multiple {
 if let Some(offset) = offset {
 if first_match.offset != offset {
 anyhow::bail!("failed to find attachment {name} at offset {offset}");
 }
 }
 return Ok(first_match);
}
```

The effect on lines 237-244 will trigger `refreshClineRecommendedModels()` every time the provider changes to "cline" or whenever the lengths of the model arrays change, even if the data hasn't actually changed. This could lead to unnecessary API calls.

Consider using a more stable approach:
1. Add a state flag to track if models have been fetched at least once
2. Only refresh if the flag is false and provider is "cline"
3. Set the flag to true after the first successful fetch

This would prevent repeated API calls when switching providers or when the component re-renders.
```suggestion
 const [hasFetchedClineModels, setHasFetchedClineModels] = useState(false)

 useMount(() => {
 refreshOpenRouterModels()
 refreshClineRecommendedModels()
 setHasFetchedClineModels(true)
 })

 useEffect(() => {
 if (modeFields.apiProvider !== "cline") {
 return
 }
 if (hasFetchedClineModels) {
 return
 }

 refreshClineRecommendedModels()
 setHasFetchedClineModels(true)
 }, [modeFields.apiProvider, hasFetchedClineModels, refreshClineRecommendedModels])
```

